  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nCurrently have a stationary front extending from southeast\nNebraska into north central and far northwest KS. Large complex\nof storms over north central/northeast NE has kicked out some\noutflow that may help reinforce the front and push it a bit more\nsouth. Front is expected to make it to just north of I-70 today\nwhich will keep all of the area in extreme heat again, with\nhighs very close to where they were yesterday. May even see\nlocations near and just south of I-70 get higher with\ncompressional heating near the front. Unfortunately we are not\nexpecting much in the way of storms along the front due to\nextreme mid mid level temps providing enough capping. Still\nlooking for an impressive low level jet to develop tonight\nacross the Plains as upper troughing digs from the Pacific\nNorthwest into Central CA. At the same time, an upper impulse\nover the Great Basin lifts out across the Central Plains. This\nwill result in an MCS tracking across Nebraska tonight.\n\nThe main question for Thursday will be how far south outflow\nfrom the MCS to the north makes it. There are a couple models\nthat push it as far south as Salina to just north of \nHutchinson. However, the majority of the models keep it further \nnorth and at this time will go with the thinking it may make it\nto just south of I-70. So there is a chance that highs could be\na bit cooler Thu for places like Russell and Great Bend with \nhigh confidence the remainder of the area has another day with \nextreme heat. There will be a chance for a few storms late Thu \nafternoon over central KS along the front with better chances \nThu evening as a weak piece of energy lifts out of NM. Extreme \nheat will be likely Fri area wide as the front lifts back to the\nnorth. Just like for Thursday, there will be uncertainty \nSaturday as the front will try to sink into northern KS again. \nGFS takes it further south compared to the ECMWF but at this \ntime feel that keeping things warm is the way to go. \n\nThere is good model agreement that a more substantial monsoonal\nimpulse will lift into the Central Rockies and across Nebraska \nSat night. This will shove the upper ridge slightly south and \nflatten out the upper flow across the central/northern Plains. \nThis will hopefully knock down highs a bit for Sun into the \nstart of the work week, so instead of highs in the 102-108 range\nthey will be in the mid to upper 90s.",
